A one-sided `forwardRef()` does not resolve a genuine cycle. `forwardRef()` defers evaluation of the referenced module/provider until after both sides are defined — but that deferral only works if **both** sides are wrapped. On the OrdersModule side you've deferred looking at PricingModule, but PricingModule is still importing OrdersModule eagerly at evaluation time, so when PricingModule's metadata is read it hits the not-yet-initialized OrdersModule and Nest reports the cycle it can't break.

Fix: apply `forwardRef()` symmetrically, on both sides of the mutual reference.

**OrdersModule:**
```ts
@Module({
  imports: [forwardRef(() => PricingModule)],
  ...
})
export class OrdersModule {}
```

**PricingModule:**
```ts
@Module({
  imports: [forwardRef(() => OrdersModule)],
  ...
})
export class PricingModule {}
```

Both module-level imports must be wrapped — not just the one you added first.

